<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish–Soviet War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Polish–Bolshevik War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish–Soviet War was fought from 1919 to 1921 between the Second Polish Republic and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> over the lands between them. After Polish forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in 1920, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> advanced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a>'s counter-offensive won the Battle of Warsaw; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Peace of Riga</a> (1921) divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and Ukraine between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the Soviet republics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-soviet-war.jpg" alt="Polish–Soviet War" width="320" height="566" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: nieznani · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polish-soviet_war_montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">Armistice of 11 November 1918</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>'s Soviet Russia annulled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> and moved forces westward to reclaim the Ober Ost regions the Germans had abandoned. Lenin saw the newly independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as a route for spreading communist revolution into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Polish leaders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a>, aimed to restore Poland's borders from before 1772. The lands in dispute, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, had once belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> and later to Poland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, and many had passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in the Partitions of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> on 5 January 1919, and throughout that year Polish forces occupied much of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and won the Polish-Ukrainian War. On 21 April 1920 Piłsudski signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> with the Ukrainian leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/symon-petliura">Symon Petliura</a>, whose government had in practice been defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>. Piłsudski then launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> offensive, and Polish and allied Ukrainian forces captured the city on 7 May 1920. The offensive lacked local support, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> joined the Red Army rather than Petliura's forces.</p><p>A Soviet counteroffensive began in June 1920, and by August the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> had pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. In the Battle of Warsaw, fought between 12 and 25 August, Polish forces won an unexpected victory, often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-warsaw-1920">Miracle on the Vistula</a>. Polish codebreakers, led by Jan Kowalewski and joined by mathematicians from Warsaw and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>, had broken Soviet ciphers, which let Polish commanders use their forces efficiently. Peace talks began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> in mid-August and moved to Riga in September, and a ceasefire took effect on 18 October 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Peace of Riga</a>, signed on 18 March 1921, set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s eastern border about 200 kilometres east of the Curzon Line. Poland received eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> and recognised the Ukrainian and Byelorussian Soviet republics, which ended Piłsudski's hope of a Polish-led federation. Of 80–85 thousand Soviet prisoners of war, 16–20 thousand died in Polish captivity, and of 51 thousand Polish prisoners, 20 thousand died. The reported pogroms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> by the Polish military led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to send a commission under Henry Morgenthau. Historians do not agree on who won: the war is seen as a Polish military victory but also as a political defeat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polish–Soviet War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a series of wars fought by the new Polish Army from 1918 to 1921: the Polish–Ukrainian War from Lwów, the Greater Poland uprising, the Polish–Czechoslovak War over Trans-Olza, the Polish–Lithuanian War, three Silesian Uprisings and, most critical, the <b>Polish–Soviet War</b> (1919–1921), which ended in a decisive Polish victory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
